message.content:
I examined enhanced close-ups of the molar regions and found two likely periapical lesions. Both are in the lower jaw. This is a visual estimate from one panoramic image, not a clinical diagnosis.

- **Lower left first molar** (image right; the tooth with the filling): there is a fairly well-defined dark area around the distal root tip and between the roots. I graded it **PAI 4**.
- **Lower right first molar** (image left; the root-canal-treated tooth with a crown): there is mild, poorly defined bone loss near the root tips, mostly the front (mesial) root. I graded it **PAI 3**. This one is borderline and could be a 2, meaning not a lesion.

I didn't see any clear periapical lesions in the upper teeth or the front teeth. Overlapping structures make the upper molar region hard to judge on a panoramic image, so a lesion there could have been missed.

